  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 244 AM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nEnsemble guidance is quite confident on upper-level troughing \nThursday to start the four-day forecast period, placing us in \nstrong southwest flow aloft through Friday morning. A decent jet\nstreak is anticipated to present over our County Warning Area \n(CWA) on the lee side of the Rossby wave. Furthermore, a surface\nlow southeast of the region should continue moving east across \nthe Central Plains. The pattern likely changes over the course \nof the day Friday as the trough propagates eastward, bringing us\ninto zonal westerly flow. Moving forward, weak ridging starts \nto develop Saturday shifting 300- 500mb flow to the northwest. \nSaid ridge should move to the east fairly quickly Sunday, \nplacing the Tri-State area right back in southwest flowing \nheading into next week. Other than Thursday (10- 25%), \nProbability of Precipitation (PoPs) is expected to be very low \n(<5%) through next weekend. There is low chance (25%) for \novernight temperatures at or below freezing Friday night. \nTherefore, frost will be possible.\n\nOverall, below average temperatures are likely as the system \naloft ushers in cool/dry air from the northwest. High \ntemperatures upper 50s into the low 60s will be likely Thursday \nand Friday. This will not last as the shift to southwesterly \nflow kicks off a warming trend that raises high temps into the \nmid/upper 60s Saturday as well as mid to upper 70s Sunday. As \nmentioned in the above section, low confidence (20-30%) exists \nof temperatures falling below freezing in some locations \nthroughout the CWA Friday night into Saturday morning. Low \ntemperatures in the mid 30s will also be possible Thursday night\nin some areas near or west of K-27. \n\nFire weather could be a concern late next week into the \nweekend, so trends regarding surface winds and relative humidity\n(RH) will need to be monitored. RH is forecasted to decrease \ninto the upper teens Saturday and Sunday in the eastern Colorado\ncounties. RH values should generally range around 20-40% during\nthe day elsewhere in the CWA. Winds originally come on strong \nThursday at 20-35 mph, gusting 30-50 mph from the north. Fridays\nwinds likely will be quite a bit weaker at 10-20 mph, also from\nthe north, gusting up to 30 mph. Saturday and Sunday will be \nthe days to keep a close eye on for potential fire weather. \nSaturday's winds will vary around the region 15-25 mph from the \nsouth and gusting 20-35 mph. To close out the weekend, Sunday's \nwinds should be quite light (5-10 mph) shifting from the \nsouthwest to southeast and gusting up to 15 mph.",
